12. Real-World Example
Consider an online shopping company that wants to introduce a new feature allowing customers to apply discount coupons during checkout.
1. The developer writes the coupon functionality and pushes the code to GitHub.
2. Jenkins automatically detects the code change and starts the pipeline.
3. The application is built and dependencies are installed.
4. Automated unit, integration and functional tests are executed.
5. If any test fails, the pipeline stops and the developer receives an alert.
6. If all tests pass, a Docker image is created.
7. The application is deployed to the staging environment.
8. The team tests the coupon functionality, checkout, payments and order confirmation.
9. After approval, the application is deployed to production.
10. Monitoring tools check application performance, errors and payment success rates.
11. Feedback is provided to the development team for further improvements.
This process ensures that the new feature reaches customers faster, with fewer errors and less risk of application failure.

13. Conclusion
Implementing DevOps practices and an automated CI/CD pipeline helps the online shopping company overcome the limitations of traditional manual software development and deployment. By integrating development, testing, deployment and monitoring into a continuous automated process, the company can release new features and bug fixes faster and more reliably.

Continuous Integration detects errors early, Continuous Delivery keeps the software ready for release, and Continuous Deployment enables automatic production releases. Automation reduces human errors, improves software quality, increases collaboration between teams and enables faster recovery from .
